# component: badge
# decisions: A11Y-13, A11Y-2, A11Y-8, API-16, COL-12, DOC-2, FOC-1, FOC-5, FOC-6
# hunks:
#   A11Y-2: an interactive badge (`[a]`/`[button]`) gets an invisible `::after` hit area and drops
#           `overflow-hidden`, so its pointer target clears 24px. Upstream's is 20px tall.
#   API-16: `"use client"` is added — `useRender` calls `React.useRef` internally, so the module
#           throws on import in an RSC without the boundary (`verify-rsc-safety.mjs`).
#   FOC-1, FOC-6: the `focus-visible:border-ring focus-visible:ring-[3px] focus-visible:ring-ring/50`
#     glow and the `aria-invalid` rings are removed; `base.css` owns focus outright.
#   FOC-5: the invalid border becomes `not-focus:aria-invalid:border-destructive`, so a focused
#     badge shows the focus cue rather than the red tint.
#   COL-12: `success`, `warning` and `info` variants in upstream's own `destructive` shape.
#   A11Y-13: each tinted status variant takes the family's `-text` ink; `bg-<family>/10` under
#     `text-<family>` measures 3.98–4.35:1, under the AA floor A11Y-1 enforces.
#   A11Y-8: a badge is a labelled chip — the tint never carries the meaning alone; asserted by
#     `badge.test.tsx` and shown on the docs page.
#   cn: `import { cn } from "cn"` becomes `@vegastack/design` — our npm layer owns `cn` (DOC-2).
#   formatting: the repository formatter (prettier defaults: semicolons, trailing commas)
#               reflows the file; those hunks carry no decision of their own.
